{
  "name": "The Green Renaissance at AKU Arusha",
  "parent_id": 203314067212801,
  "group_id": "66d3d366-7f82-478c-b714-a13720ef5cbd",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-31T09:56:25.445Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-07-31T09:56:25.528Z",
  "published_at": "2026-07-31T09:56:25.513Z",
  "id": 203989751618844,
  "uuid": "69e73c3b-e017-494a-8fa9-38444bc1841b",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "355e98a4-64d6-b404-1392-9d747c33dbba",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "30a30f80-1e46-4cef-c5e0-89d5e57b8885",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Bernad Emmanuel, the dedicated site foreman, led a team with a vision: to breathe life into every corner of the campus. Their mission in 2025 was ambitious-restore, renew, and reimagine.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Chapter One: Gardens of Hope",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The first chapter of this story unfolded in the gardens. Near the Community Outreach Centre, a micro forest of 312 trees rose like green sentinels, promising shade and cleaner air for generations to come. Flower beds bloomed along the tent roads, and Jacaranda Garden became a haven of vegetables, nourished by a newly installed irrigation system. The campus was no longer just a place of learning-it was becoming a living ecosystem.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Chapter Two: Breathing Life into Old Walls",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "But the transformation didn't stop at greenery. Old structures whispered stories of neglect, and the team listened. Lakilaki House, idle for over 10 years, was reborn-renovated, fenced, and fitted with a powerful pump system. The canteen, once cramped for 30, now welcomed 70 hungry minds. Magongo House gained a new well and sturdy pumps, ensuring water flowed like hope through its veins. Seven grand tents rose at Manyara House, ready to shelter field researchers under the African sky. The Tent city now have 12 semi luxury tents.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Chapter Three: Details that Matter",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Every detail mattered. Wooden strips guarded walls from chair scratches, a large drift tamed unruly rainwater, and even the swimming pool found new purpose under a concrete cover. Fresh paint brightened the ACER offices, signaling a new era of care.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Chapter Four: A Campus Reimagined",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "bold"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Repairs stitched the campus together like a skilled tailor. Roads were smoothed, potholes filled, and electrical systems revived. A 170,000-liter fire water tank stood sentinel at Manyara Garden, flanked by two powerful pumps-a fortress against flames.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "By year's end, AKU Arusha wasn't just maintained; it was transformed. It stood as a testament to what vision, teamwork, and respect for nature could achieve-a campus where learning thrived alongside life.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
